<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kordofan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kordofan is a region of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> lying between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> valley. It was a contested borderland over which the Darfur Sultanate and its eastern neighbours fought for control of trade and resources. Darfur's loss of Kordofan to Egyptian forces in 1821 was a significant blow that foreshadowed the sultanate's later decline.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kordofan.jpg" alt="Kordofan" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown Vectorization: Carnby · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_North_Kurdufan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kordofan is a region in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-sudan">central Sudan</a> covering some 376,145 square kilometres. It is largely an undulating plain, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuba-mountains">Nuba Mountains</a> in the southeast. The rainy season from June to September makes the area fertile, but in the dry season it is virtually desert. The region is known for gum arabic, and its crops include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundnut">groundnuts</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a>. Its chief town is El Obeid. Kordofan was originally inhabited by Nubian-speaking peoples and in antiquity was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kingdom of Kush</a>, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> of the Nubian kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alodia">Alodia</a> and Makuria. By the 14th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> were expanding southwards into it, and most of its inhabitants are now Arabs, among them the semi-nomadic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baggara-arabs">Baggara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a>-raising Kababish.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>For centuries Kordofan largely escaped control by any one of the region's major states, though the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/funj-sultanate-of-sennar">Funj Sultanate of Sennar</a> had influence over its east. In 1734 Isawi of the Musabba'at, an exiled faction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur</a>'s royal family, dislodged the Funj administration there. Abu Likaylik later conquered it, and about 1772 Hashim of the Musabba'at led an insurgency that retook it. The Funj ousted Hashim in 1780 and held Kordofan for about five years. After a decisive defeat of Sennar in 1784 the country was under Darfur viceroys and enjoyed prosperity: tribute was a voluntary present to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Darfur, merchants paid no duties, and caravans brought goods from Abyssinia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> to El Obeid and Bara.</p><p>This ended in 1821, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt">Muhammad Ali Pasha</a>, Ottoman viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, sent his son-in-law Mahommed Bey the Defturdar with about 4,500 soldiers and eight pieces of artillery to subject Kordofan; the monopoly of the Egyptian governors then impeded trade. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a> captured El Obeid in 1883, and an Egyptian force under the British general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-hicks">William Hicks</a> was ambushed and annihilated at Sheikan to its south. After the British reoccupation in 1898 Kordofan became one of the provinces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kordofan was split in 1973 into the provinces of North Kordofan and South Kordofan, which became states in 1994 together with West Kordofan. West Kordofan was abolished in August 2005, under the Comprehensive Peace Agreement, and re-established in July 2013. Its population was estimated at 3.6 million in 2000. Armed conflict broke out in South Kordofan in June 2011, ahead of the independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-sudan">South Sudan</a>, and the war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> from 2023 brought fighting to the region again.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kordofan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nubian was spoken in central Sudan until the 19th century, Sudanese Arabic keeps many Nubian words, and rituals of Christian origin, chiefly the marking of crosses on people and objects, were still common in Omdurman, the Gezira and <b>Kordofan</b> in the 20th century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alodia">Alodia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The system worked well during the period of expansion, when Sultan Mohammed Tayrab (r. c. 1752–1785) campaigned eastward and annexed <b>Kordofan</b> in the 1780s, extending the sultanate's reach towards the Nile Valley and its trade routes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/darfur-sultanate">Darfur Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1744 Funj forces defeated an invasion by the Ethiopian emperor Iyasu II on the Dinder River, and in the late 1740s the general Muhammad Abu Likaylik conquered <b>Kordofan</b> from its Musabba’at rulers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/funj-sultanate">Funj Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Traditions say the Daju were displaced by the Tunjur around the 14th or 15th century, and Daju groups moved south and west, where Daju-speaking communities still live in Darfur, <b>Kordofan</b> and Chad.</blockquote><a href="/state/daju-kingdom">Daju kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>The Musabba'at were a branch of the ruling Keira family of Darfur who moved east into <b>Kordofan</b> in the 17th century after a dynastic dispute.</blockquote><a href="/state/musabbaat-sultanate">Musabba'at sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1820 to 1821 armies sent by Egypt under Muhammad Ali, led by his son Ismail Pasha, conquered the Funj Sultanate and <b>Kordofan</b>, seeking gold and slaves for the army.</blockquote><a href="/state/turco-egyptian-sudan">Turco-Egyptian Sudan</a></li></ul></section>
